 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of how lookup is implemented. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index building, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups are served from a local embedded SQLite index stored on the kiosk. The index file, its schema, and its location are private to this node.
+
+## Index
+
+Each snapshot is built into its own SQLite database file. The file holds one row per record, keyed by the normalized serial number, plus the snapshot date and schema version of the snapshot it was built from. Because the snapshot date travels inside the index file, a lookup result can never pair records from one snapshot with the date of another.
+
+The index is a derived artifact. It is always rebuilt from a validated snapshot and is never edited in place, which keeps registry records read-only on the kiosk.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness, all before the snapshot is allowed to replace the active index.
+
+Build the new index in a staging file beside the active one, on the same volume. Lookups continue to be served from the active index while the build runs. When the build completes and every validation has passed, activate the new index with a single atomic replacement, so a lookup sees either the previous snapshot in full or the new one in full.
+
+An import failure at any step (signature, schema version, duplicate serial numbers, a malformed record, removed media, insufficient disk space) discards the staging file, leaves the previous index active, and reports an error that names the failing step. A staging file left behind by an interrupted import is discarded at the next start and is never activated.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Look the normalized serial number up as an exact key match in the active index, which is opened read-only. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+A keyed read of one row from a 90,000-row index sits well inside the 250 ms lookup goal and does not need the index held in memory.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Kiosks have no network route during normal operation and every lookup must complete from files on the kiosk, which rules out both networked alternatives. SQLite needs no running service and gives predictable keyed lookups; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on every snapshot. |
+| Index lifecycle | Build a fresh index file per snapshot and swap it in atomically | Update the active index in place inside one transaction | A separate file keeps the previous snapshot untouched until the new one is fully validated, so a failed or interrupted import cannot damage it. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether two snapshot records whose serial numbers differ only by letter case or surrounding whitespace count as duplicates.
+2. What `findBySerial` returns before any snapshot has been imported on a kiosk.
+3. Whether a validly signed snapshot older than the active one is accepted.
+4. Whether the previous index is kept after a successful activation, and what happens when the active index is unreadable at start.
